WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.860
As we take a tour of the Redwood National Park in Northern California,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.540
we are bound to be awestruck by a mere glance of the giant redwood trees.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:20.980
Their height of over 380 feet makes them the world's tallest trees.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.320
But ever wondered how they maintain the uniformity?

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:30.040
How is it that a Redwood tree grows to an unimaginable height,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.120
but still has the same wood and similar leaves throughout?

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.960
Let me give you another example.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.700
Take the case of human beings. If we are wounded, then

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.880
the affected area gets healed in a few days.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.580
And we can see that the newer skin is exactly the same as the previous one.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.880
How is all this possible?

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.260
It is because of the process of cell division.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.900
This is an extremely interesting process in which

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.080
the cell makes almost an exact copy of itself.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.200
And how is it able to make its replica?

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.340
All thanks to the process of DNA replication.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.500
With this the cell is able to make a copy of itself.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.980
We know that DNA encodes everything.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.000
So when the cell is about to divide, the replication of DNA

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.980
helps in conserving the genetic information.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:32.660
As a result, the newly formed cells carry the same genetic information.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.700
Thus, in order to have similar or identical cells,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:43.000
the process of DNA replication and cell division are extremely important.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.140
Let's understand how this miraculous process of DNA replication works.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.780
Way back in time, there were three major hypotheses

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.580
of the methods of replication put forth.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.700
One method is called the conservative method.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.640
The other is called the dispersive method,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.520
and the last one is called the semi-conservative method.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.280
But which of the methods describe the replication process correctly?

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.540
Which method is used by DNA to make copies of itself?

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.840
Let's find that out.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.920
But before we get to the answer,

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.220
we need to understand the structure of a typical DNA molecule.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.680
This is how it looks.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.800
This is the backbone of the DNA molecule

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.340
and this part forms the core.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.440
Now let's understand a few more interesting concepts

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.900
regarding the DNA molecule.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.960
Let's begin with the backbone first.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.860
Can you guess how these two strands are arranged?

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.340
Is there any way to differentiate between these two?

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:54.680
Yes, there is. The two complimentary DNA strands are antiparallel.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.020
And what does this mean?

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.360
Well, in biochemistry two units like these strands,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.340
are said to be antiparallel if they run parallel to each other

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.060
but in the opposite direction.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:14.720
So, is there directionality that is end to end alignment in the case of DNA?

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.940
Yes, there is.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:23.420
Let's understand the directionality in the DNA molecule better in the next part.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.600
Here is a typical structure of a DNA molecule.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.000
To understand how exactly it replicates

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.340
we need to first understand its detailed structure.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.760
To begin with let's zoom in further.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.020
We find a structure somewhat like this.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.200
These are the representations of the nitrogenous bases,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.960
while these are that of the sugar phosphate backbones.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.880
Can you state the difference between these two backbones?

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.659
It's quite simple.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:04.340
If you notice properly, the ribose sugars in this strand

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.420
are pointing upwards,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.000
while here they're facing downwards.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.060
To be precise, the oxygen atoms in these sugar molecules

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.040
are headed upwards, while in these ones they're headed downwards.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.640
So doesn't it seem that these two strands are running in opposite directions?

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.620
Yes, they are.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.780
This is why the two strands are said to be antiparallel.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:35.040
Is this the only reason why we say that the two strands are antiparallel?

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.800
Actually not.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.540
There is a specific way to read the directions of the two strands.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.480
And that comes not only from the sugar molecules,

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.400
but also from the phosphate groups.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.880
Let's understand how.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.740
We note that the phosphate groups are attached to the sugars

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.560
with the help of phosphodiester linkages.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:03.220
Now the interesting part is that these linkages occur at specific positions only.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.060
In every sugar molecule the phosphate groups are attached

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.180
at the third carbon atom and the fifth carbon atom only.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.600
So in this strand of DNA,

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.240
we can see that this phosphate group

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.140
is attached to this fifth carbon atom of the sugar molecule,

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:24.020
while this phosphate is attached to the third.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:29.100
Also, it's attached to the fifth carbon atom of the next sugar molecule.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.140
This is how the phosphate groups link the sugar molecules together

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.340
at the fifth and the third carbon atoms.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.140
So for a layman, this strand would look like this.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.480
A phosphate group, fifth carbon atom, third carbon atom,

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:49.880
phosphate group,  fifth carbon atom, third carbon atom and so on.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.180
So the fifth carbon atom to which the phosphate group is attached

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.480
is called five prime carbon.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.860
And the third carbon atom to which a free hydroxyl group is attached

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.140
is called three prime carbon.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:10.880
To simplify this, in a DNA strand, this end is referred to as the five prime end,

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.680
while this is the three prime end.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.880
But why is the term prime used here?

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.200
It's simply to distinguish these carbon atoms

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:25.600
from the ones which are involved in linkages with the nitrogenous bases.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.980
Getting back to our explanation this is the five prime end

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.500
while this is the three prime end.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.800
The five prime end has the phosphate group attached to it,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.860
while the three prime end has a free hydroxyl group.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:47.180
So the direction of the strand can be read as five prime to three prime.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.920
From the free phosphate group to the free hydroxyl group.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.280
Now can you guess the direction of the other strand?

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.640
Give it a shot.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:04.800
That's correct the direction of this strand will be read as three prime to five prime.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.700
That's because in this strand, it begins with a 3 prime end

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.880
having the free hydroxyl group,

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.940
and ends with the 5 prime end having the phosphate group.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:19.000
This is how the two strands are said to be antiparallel

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.460
with respect to each other.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:26.980
Now tell me how is this complex looking DNA molecule replicated?

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:30.160
We had seen that there were three theories put forth

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.480
to understand the DNA replication process.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:39.660
They were the conservative, dispersive, and semiconservative theory.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.760
But which amongst the three is the correct one?

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:48.900
Let's understand this in the upcoming parts.

